contentText:
Another Future album, another No. 1 on the Billboard 200 .

Like 11 previous sets released by the superstar rapper — including all seven of his prior official non-collaboration albums —  The Real Me , released on July 10, debuts at No. 1 on this week&#8217;s chart (dated July 25). The set bows with 131,000 first-week units, while also charting 17 songs on the Billboard Hot 100 .

Those numbers are down a little from Future&#8217;s last unaccompanied solo album, however, as 2022&#8217;s I Never Liked You debuted with 222,000 units. That set also littered the Hot 100, with eight of its tracks entered in the top 20 — while the top-ranking Real Me cut on this week&#8217;s chart is &#8220;California Girls&#8221; at No. 23.

Are those returns about what we expected for this Future set in its first week? And is The Real Me properly reflective as a title of the album&#8217;s lyrical content? Billboard staffers discuss these questions and more below.

1.&nbsp; The Real&nbsp;Me&nbsp; debuts atop the Billboard 200 this week with 131,000 first-week album units. Is that number lower, higher, or about what you would have expected? &nbsp;

Kyle Denis : Considering the muted reception of “Radio” and the lack of any real conversation or hype leading up to the album release, 131,000&nbsp;first-week&nbsp;units is about what I would have expected for&nbsp; The Real Me .&nbsp;

Angel Diaz : I guess&nbsp;it&#8217;s&nbsp;right around where I expected it to be.&nbsp;He also&nbsp;didn’t&nbsp;have much to compete with and had a&nbsp;pretty clear&nbsp;runway to snag his&nbsp;12th&nbsp;No. 1 album.&nbsp; The Real Me &nbsp;essentially did&nbsp; the same first week numbers &nbsp;as&nbsp; Mixtape Pluto .

Carl Lamarre : Maybe slightly lower, but I’m not totally surprised. I probably would’ve guessed somewhere in the ballpark of 150,000–160,000 units based on name recognition alone. But I think we&nbsp;have to remember that the build-up for Future wasn’t as robust as in years past. With&nbsp; We Don’t Trust You , fans gravitated toward the magnetism of him and Metro Boomin teaming up, while “Like That” became an explosive Hot 100 chart-topper thanks to Kendrick Lamar’s verse. Before that,&nbsp; I Never Liked You &nbsp;was powered by “Wait For U” with Drake and Tems.&nbsp; The Real Me &nbsp;leaned more into mystery, and while “Radio” was a solid record, it wasn’t enough to generate the same level of buzz. The lack of features this time around also certainly played a role in the drop in numbers.

Michael Saponara : Even though it’s Future’s first album in about two years, I felt like his first-week sales total is only a smidge lower than expected. Without a potent lead single and keeping the project guest-free, I looked for &nbsp;The Real Me&nbsp; to mirror a sum closer to&nbsp; Mixtape Pluto ’s 129,000 units than&nbsp; We Don’t Trust You ’s 251,000.&nbsp;

Andrew Unterberger : It&#8217;s a little low. Six figures for a hip-hop album without a physical release is still pretty solid in 2026, but Future&#8217;s always been able to create genuine heat with his new official LP releases until this point —&nbsp;I&#8217;m not sure I&#8217;m feeling that with this one, either in the numbers or just through anecdotal observation.

2. Both Future’s most recent unaccompanied album, 2022’s&nbsp; I Never Liked You , and the first of his two 2024 team-up albums with Metro Boomin,&nbsp; We&nbsp;Don’t&nbsp;Trust You , moved comfortably over 200,000 units in their first week. What do you think is the biggest reason for the&nbsp;drop in&nbsp;units for&nbsp; The Real Me ’s debut frame? &nbsp;

Kyle Denis : I Never Liked You&nbsp; had the post-Valentine&#8217;s Day glow of lead single “Worst Day” and an immediate smash hit in “Wait For U” (with Drake & Tems), while&nbsp; We Don’t Trust You &nbsp;arrived at the beginning of the biggest rap beef in a quarter-century, complete with its own smash hit in the Kendrick Lamar-assisted “Like That.” Simply put,&nbsp; The Real Me &nbsp;had none of that — it was&nbsp;always going to be a challenge for an album with no narrative and no hits (yet?)&nbsp;to&nbsp;match those two.&nbsp;

Angel Diaz : There wasn’t really a lead up single going into the release and I think some folks just might have Future fatigue.&nbsp;You also&nbsp;have to&nbsp;factor in the hype that&nbsp;the&nbsp;first Metro&nbsp;tape&nbsp;had,&nbsp;and then&nbsp;you&nbsp;have to&nbsp;factor in&nbsp;the fever&nbsp;pitch&nbsp;the second one reached after&nbsp;the noise the&nbsp;previous&nbsp;release made. I think&nbsp;it&#8217;s&nbsp;a bit unfair to compare that project (I view it as a double disc)&nbsp;to this.

Carl Lamarre : The storyline just wasn’t quite there for Pluto this go-round. Without a compelling narrative like&nbsp; We Don’t Trust You &nbsp;or a blockbuster single on the level of “Wait For U,” there simply wasn’t the same anticipation surrounding this album cycle. Aside from a few tweets and social posts, Future never really sold fans on the premise of&nbsp; The Real Me ,&nbsp;or why this was an album that demanded their full attention.

Michael Saponara : Adding onto my answer from the first question, I’d lean on there not being a better lead single to add anticipation for the project, along with the album being a solo endeavor without any of his A-list friends making appearances.&nbsp; We Don’t Trust You &nbsp;was heavily anticipated as a full project, building on his established rapport with Metro Boomin and inviting titans to the party like Kendrick Lamar and The Weeknd.&nbsp;

Andrew Unterberger : I think we&#8217;re just in different times for hip-hop now than we were in 2022, and maybe even in 2024 —&nbsp;without a major narrative hook or proven hit to anchor a project, long-established stars can&#8217;t walk into automatically dominant first weeks the way they used to.

3. “California Girls” is the&nbsp;highest-charting&nbsp;of Future’s 18 entries on the Hot 100 this week, bowing at No. 23. Does it seem like a&nbsp;lasting&nbsp;hit to you? If not, do you think&nbsp;there’s&nbsp;another one to be found elsewhere on the album? &nbsp;

Kyle Denis : I&#8230; do not remember what “California Girls” sounds like. But I do recall “Tank Top Pluto,” “Build&nbsp;A B-ch,” “2018” and “Hollywood,” and I think at least&nbsp;one&nbsp;of those four will be the album’s true lasting hit.&nbsp;

Angel Diaz : I think&nbsp;we’ll&nbsp;be hearing about “California Girls” for the rest of the summer. It has all the makings of a Future Hot 100 hit.&nbsp;It’s&nbsp;catchy and&nbsp;it’s&nbsp;already making an impact on social media. “Konnichiwa,”&nbsp;“Hollywood”&nbsp;and “Alice” are the sleepers, though. All three of those are&nbsp;a viral&nbsp;video and commercial&nbsp;placement&nbsp;away from being&nbsp;certified&nbsp;hits.&nbsp;“F—k&nbsp;an&nbsp;Interview” and&nbsp;“2018”&nbsp;have been my personal favorites, so far.

Carl Lamarre : This is personally one of my favorites because it taps into the&nbsp; HNDRXX &nbsp;fabric. Future’s dreamy exploits and lustful intentions have often been a winning formula. It’s toxic, sure, but it also feels tailor-made for Instagram baddies looking for the soundtrack to their summer mischief. If you’re looking for something a little more turnt up, though, “Tank Top Pluto” is a distant second and has serious heat-rock potential.

Michael Saponara : It doesn’t seem like there will be a long-lasting hit from&nbsp; The Real Me &nbsp;sticking around the top 40 for months down the road. Future flooding the Hot 100 with 17 entries (18 if you count his feature appearance on Drake&#8217;s &#8220;Ran to Atlanta&#8221;) is par for the course with A-list acts landing most, if not all of their albums onto the chart the week after its arrival.

Andrew Unterberger : I wouldn&#8217;t bet on &#8220;California Girls&#8221; or any other song having an extended chart impact. Look at Spotify&#8217;s Daily Top Songs USA chart right now and you won&#8217;t find a Future song in the entire top…
